risecam.app,0,"RiseCam — Time-lapse the work, not just the win RiseCam quietly time-lapses your work sessions and hands you a 30-second video of everything you got done. Free on the App Store. RiseCam — Time-lapse the work, not just the win RiseCam Features How it works Screens FAQ Get the app 🇬🇧 EN 🇬🇧 English 🇷🇺 Русский Coming soon to iOS Time-lapse the work, not just the win. Prop your phone up and press record. RiseCam grabs a frame every few seconds while your hands are busy — painting, practising, lifting, studying, sorting the whole cupboard out. Hit stop and you have a 30-second video of the effort nobody usually sees. Coming soon to the App Store Free to start Recording works offline Frames stay on your phone REC Every 5s Finish the lino print 00:12:40 152 frames Session saved 2h 06m of effort → 30s video What it does A camera that only cares about effort No streaks to protect, no leaderboard, no nagging notifications. Just a record button and something to show afterwards. Set-and-forget capture Prop the phone up and tap record. RiseCam takes a frame every five seconds — or whatever interval suits the job — and never asks for your attention again. Attached to real activities Every session belongs to an activity, so your library reads like a work log instead of a camera roll you are afraid to open. Sessions that count themselves Start and stop is the entire interface. Elapsed time and frame count are recorded for you, no timers to remember. Proof you can look back on Per-activity stats show how many sessions you ran and how long you actually spent — the number, not the vibe. One-tap export Pick 15, 30 or 60 seconds. RiseCam re-times your frames and writes an MP4 straight to your photo library. Offline first Recording never waits on a network. Sessions sync in the background once you are back on Wi-Fi. How it works Three taps, then get on with it Pick the activity Choose what you are about to work on, or add it in two taps. That name ends up on the session and the export. Prop it up and record Any angle works — desk, easel, workbench, kitchen counter. Tap record and forget the phone is there. Collect the receipt Stop when you are done. Two hours of quiet work becomes a 30-second video you can keep, post, or send to someone who asked. Inside the app Small enough to actually use Four screens, one job. Pick an activity, record, and export what you made. Activities New Rewrite the onboarding doc 3 sessions · 2h 06m Sand and oil the shelf 5 sessions · 4h 12m Learn the F barre chord 12 sessions · 3h 40m Tax paperwork 1 session · 48m Repot the monstera 2 sessions · 51m Portfolio case study 7 sessions · 6h 18m Your activities, newest first Timelapses 0:30 0:15 1:00 0:30 0:15 0:30 Every session, kept as a clip Export Video Length 15s 30s 60s Frames 152 Resolution 1080p Watermark Free Save to Photos Re-timed and saved in one tap Questions The short answers Do I need an account to record? No. Install it, pick an activity and record. An account is only there for when you want your activities and sessions on more than one device. Can I change how often it takes a frame? Yes — five seconds is the default, and you can set a different interval in Settings. Longer intervals mean smaller sessions and punchier time-lapses. Is there a watermark on exports? Free exports carry a small mark in the corner naming the app and how long the session ran — ""2h 06m of real work · RiseCam"". Free exports are also capped at 15 seconds. Pro removes both. Which devices does it run on? iPhone and iPad, on iOS 26.5 or later. There is no Android app yet. Start your next session with the camera on Free to download, free to record, and the first export takes about ten seconds. Your desk is already set up for it. Coming soon to the App Store RiseCam Time-lapse the work, not just the win. Made for anyone who finishes things and has nothing to show for it. Product Features How it works Screens FAQ Support [email protected] Help & answers Legal Privacy Terms Analytics choice Language 🇬🇧 English 🇷🇺 Русский © 2026 RiseCam. All rights reserved. Built in Angular, rendered on the server. Sign in"
