terrygoodkindlegacy.com,0,"Terry Goodkind — Your life is yours alone A tribute to Terry Goodkind, who wrote the Sword of Truth: a world with laws, a sword that answers to truth, and one sentence worth keeping. Your life is yours alone. Rise up and live it. Terry Goodkind — Your life is yours alone Skip to the page Your life is yours alone. Rise up and live it. Terry Goodkind The Gift The World of the Living The Rules The Legacy The Books Chapters I The Gift II The World of the Living III The Rules IV The Legacy V The Books 1948 – 2020 Terry Goodkind He wrote a mountain with a fortress cut into it, a sword that answers to truth, and magic that obeys laws the way stone obeys weight. He wrote a boundary made out of the world of the dead, and a palace so large that it is a city standing on a plateau. He wrote women whose touch takes a person’s will and leaves only the truth, and eleven rules about the way people think that need no magic at all. Why this site exists A door standing open This is a tribute to a storyteller, and it hopes for one thing. Somewhere in the next few screens you should set this aside and go open one of his books. Everything here is a door, and the door is standing open. Terry Goodkind wrote the Sword of Truth — eleven novels beginning with Wizard’s First Rule in 1994 — and around them a shelf of prequels, sequels and companions that runs to thirty-two books. They sold more than twenty-five million copies in more than twenty languages. That is the small part of it. The large part is that readers who found these books at fifteen were still quoting them at forty. They quote the rules. They quote a paragraph about a speck of time. Something in the work went in deep and stayed there. He built the magic the way he built cabinets, and it holds. Additive magic adds to what is already there. Subtractive magic takes it away. Wizard’s fire is heat drawn out of the air and piled into a shape. The Boundary between the lands is the world of the dead, stood up on its edge by wizards who needed a lock and had nothing else strong enough to make one. Magic with laws means choices with weight, and nothing in these books is ever solved by a wish. Weight is what lets a story mean something. His are about a person who thinks, who loves, who builds, and who keeps his own life as his own. He called them “the American ideal of life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness.” He also said it shorter, and he said it twice: once cut into the base of a sundial in Faith of the Fallen , and once at the close of Confessor , where the last thing Richard does is abolish the kneeling — there will be no more devotions; none of you have to kneel before me or anyone else — and then say the ten words readers have carried out of the books ever since: Your life is yours alone. Rise up and live it. This is the Grace, drawn in sorcerer’s sand. The star at the center is the gift. The inner circle is the world of the living . The square is the veil . The outer circle is the underworld . And the rays pass from the star outward through all of it, and keep going. Which is the same claim as the sentence above, drawn instead of written. The gift passes through the world of the living. It is here, and it is yours, and it does not stop at the edge of what you can see. The five chapters of this site are its five parts, read from the center outward. the star · the gift The Gift The man. A boy with dyslexia who told himself stories because the page fought him, who grew up to build cabinets and violins and paint the sea, and who wrote his first novel at forty-five. the inner circle · the living The World of the Living The Keep, the Boundary, Agaden Reach, and the people who walk them. A world with weather in it, and roads you can feel underfoot. the square · the veil The Rules Eleven Wizard’s Rules, one to a novel: hard-won teachings, and every one of them still good on a Tuesday afternoon in this world. the outer circle · what came after The Legacy What was said when he died, by a novelist, a bookshop and a friend. The last book he saw published, and the ones that kept coming without him. the rays · onward The Books The whole shelf, in order, with every ending left intact. And a straight answer to the only question t"
