thesupremecitizens.org,0,"The Supreme Citizens Charter | Constitutional Citizenship The Supreme Citizens Charter is a civic and educational framework promoting constitutional citizenship, equal civic dignity, public stewardship, accountable government, and the principle that We the People are sovereign. The Supreme Citizens Charter | Constitutional Citizenship Skip to content WE THE PEOPLE CONSTITUTIONAL CITIZENSHIP PUBLIC STEWARDSHIP THE SUPREME CITIZENS CHARTER A Civic & Educational Initiative ☰ Home The Charter 12 Principles Supreme Citizen Citizens Branch Participate Connect With Congress Voting Founding Documents About A civic identity grounded in constitutional self-government We are all Supreme Citizens. Not because any one citizen stands above another, but because together we are We the People —the source of legitimate political authority in a constitutional republic. Read the Charter What is a Supreme Citizen? Equal citizenship. Constitutional limits. Accountable government. Peaceful civic participation. Official Government Sources TODAY IN YOUR GOVERNMENT Loading latest official government information... WHITE HOUSE Executive Branch ● Official source: WhiteHouse.gov Loading latest Presidential Actions... Visit WhiteHouse.gov U.S. CONGRESS Legislative Branch ● Official source: Congress.gov Loading latest legislative activity... Visit Congress.gov U.S. SUPREME COURT Judicial Branch ● Official source: SupremeCourt.gov Loading latest opinions and orders... Visit SupremeCourt.gov “ We do not seek citizens above the law. We seek equal citizens protected by and responsible under the law. The revised inclusive identity Citizenship as stewardship The Supreme Citizens identity frames citizenship as an enduring constitutional relationship—not a partisan label, social rank, or claim of personal sovereignty. A Supreme Citizen participates within lawful constitutional government, protects the rights of others, questions power, seeks truth, rejects political violence, and helps preserve the Republic for future generations. The term Supreme Citizens refers to the collective popular sovereignty of the people. The singular a Supreme Citizen expresses the responsibilities each person carries within that shared civic body. Explore the civic identity → Equality, not hierarchy No Supreme Citizen is supreme over another. The Charter explicitly rejects personal exemption from courts, taxes, licenses, public obligations, or governmental jurisdiction. Participation, not exemption. Accountability, not anarchy. Rule of law, not personal sovereignty. The constitutional foundation The 12 Principles Twelve principles organize the Charter around popular sovereignty, equal citizenship, constitutional limits, accountable institutions, civic participation, and responsibility to posterity. 01 Popular Sovereignty Legitimate political authority ultimately originates with the people. 02 Equal Citizenship Every citizen possesses equal civic dignity. Political disagreement does not diminish citizenship. 03 Constitutional Supremacy No public official, institution, party, movement, corporation, or citizen stands above the Constitution. 04 Distributed Constitutional Power Legislative, executive, and judicial powers are divided so that no branch becomes the government itself. 05 Unitary but Limited Executive Authority The President leads the executive branch, while Congress, the judiciary, the states, elections, and constitutional rights remain independent limits. 06 Temporary Public Stewardship Public office is a temporary public trust—not personal ownership, dominion, or sovereignty. 07 Authority Requires Accountability The greater the authority entrusted to an officeholder or institution, the greater the obligation to accept responsibility for its use. 08 Protected Liberty and Rule of Law Citizens and officials alike remain subject to lawful constitutional authority, and rights apply even when their exercise is unpopular. 09 Checks, Balances, and Institutional Independence Independent institutions and constitutional checks are safeguards against concentrated power. 10 Electoral and Civic Accountability Citizenship does not begin and end at the ballot box; self-government requires engagement before, during, and after elections. 11 Constitution Before Party P"
